# Overview
#
# An intensifier is the grammar term for an adverb that strengthens a
# claim (`extremely`, `significantly`). An adverb is a word that
# describes a verb, an adjective, or a whole sentence, such as `safely`
# or `also`. An intensifier raises a claim without measuring it, so the
# reader cannot tell how much the word adds. The fix drops the word
# (`significantly faster` becomes `faster`), or gives a number that
# stays true, such as a limit that a protocol sets. The rule sits at
# Recommended, because `significantly` also matches a statistical claim.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` lists each intensifier as one word. `highly` skips a following
# `available`.
#
# False Positives
#
# `significantly` also fires on a statistical claim (`differs
# significantly at p < 0.01`). No guard tells the two apart, and the
# adjective (`statistically significant`) passes.
#
# `highly available` names a redundant deployment, so it passes.
# `exponentially` stays out, because each of its 3 corpus hits states a
# growth rate (`increase exponentially by 2x`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Optional ProseIntensifier holds the intensifiers that sometimes
# carry meaning, such as `actually`. The Recommended ProseIntensifier
# holds every other intensifier.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 85 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 19 of 20 sampled
# hits read as well without the word. The misfire was `highly utilized
# nodes`, where the word carries the meaning.
expr main [extremely, incredibly, significantly, dramatically, drastically, greatly, vastly, tremendously, massively, hugely, substantially, considerably, enormously, immensely, exceedingly, (highly !available)]
let message "Intensifier. Delete the word, or give a number that stays true, such as a protocol limit."
let description "Flags adverbs that intensify a claim without measuring it."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The rewrite halves memory use."
allows "The difference is statistically significant at p < 0.05."
allows "The cluster is highly available."
